---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import ServiceCard from '../components/ServiceCard.astro';
import { services } from '../data/services';
---

<PageLayout
  title="Getting started"
  description="Three steps to your first running stack: prepare storage, fill in one settings file, start a stack with Docker Compose."
>
  <p>
    This guide does not hide anything behind install scripts. Every app lives in its own folder under
    <code>stacks/</code>, and every line of its Compose file has a comment that explains what it does. You will:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Prepare storage</strong>: one <code>/data</code> folder for all media and downloads.</li>
    <li><strong>Fill in settings</strong>: copy <code>stacks/.env.example</code> to <code>stacks/.env</code> and set your user ID, time zone and passwords.</li>
    <li><strong>Start a stack</strong>: go into a stack folder and run <code>docker compose up -d</code>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    You need a Linux machine with Docker Engine and the Compose plugin installed. If you do not have one yet,
    <a href="https://github.com/Zamiul-rashid/Homelabbing/blob/main/docs/03-your-first-server.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Your first server</a>
    walks through it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="prepare-storage">1. Clone the repository and prepare <code>/data</code></h2>
  <p>
    Clone the repository onto your server. Then create one storage tree under <code>/data</code> (or any path you
    prefer) and give it to your own Linux user, so containers can read and write without "permission denied" errors.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    title="Clone and create the storage tree"
    code={`git clone https://github.com/Zamiul-rashid/Homelabbing.git\ncd Homelabbing\n\n# Folders for movies, TV, music, books, photos, downloads and cloud files\nsudo mkdir -p /data/media/{movies,tv,music,books,comics,photos} /data/downloads/{incomplete,complete} /data/nextcloud\n\n# Make your user the owner\nsudo chown -R $USER:$USER /data`}
  />
  <div class="callout not-prose">
    <p class="font-semibold">Why one <code class="inline-code">/data</code> folder?</p>
    <p class="mt-1 text-text-muted">
      When downloads and the media library sit on the same disk under one parent folder, Radarr and Sonarr can
      import a finished download as a hardlink: the file appears in your library instantly and uses no extra space.
      If they live on different disks, every import is a full copy.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="settings">2. Fill in <code>stacks/.env</code></h2>
  <p>
    Every stack reads shared settings (time zone, user and group IDs, database passwords) from one file,
    <code>stacks/.env</code>. Copy the template, check your IDs, then edit it.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    title="Create your settings file"
    code={`# Copy the documented template\ncp stacks/.env.example stacks/.env\n\n# Your user and group IDs (usually 1000)\nid -u\nid -g\n\n# Set PUID, PGID, TZ and passwords\nnano stacks/.env`}
  />
  <p>Never commit <code>stacks/.env</code> to Git. It holds your passwords.</p>

  <h2 id="start">3. Start a stack</h2>
  <p>
    You do not need to start everything at once. Go into one stack folder, such as <code>media-server</code>,
    and start it in the background:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock title="Start Jellyfin" code={`cd stacks/media-server\ndocker compose up -d`} />
  <p>Docker pulls the image the first time, then prints something like this:</p>
  <pre class="font-mono text-sm"><code>[+] Running 2/2
 ✔ Network media-server_default  Created
 ✔ Container jellyfin            Started</code></pre>
  <p>
    Open <code>http://YOUR_SERVER_IP:8096</code> in a browser and the Jellyfin setup wizard appears. Replace
    <code>YOUR_SERVER_IP</code> with the address of your server on your home network (<code>hostname -I</code> shows it).
  </p>

  <h2 id="troubleshooting">If something goes wrong</h2>
  <p>Most first-day problems are one of these four.</p>
  <dl>
    <dt>The page does not load</dt>
    <dd>
      Run <code>docker compose ps</code> in the stack folder. If the container is restarting, read its log with
      <code>docker compose logs -f</code>. The last few lines usually name the problem.
    </dd>
    <dt>"Permission denied" in the logs</dt>
    <dd>
      <code>PUID</code> and <code>PGID</code> in <code>stacks/.env</code> must match <code>id -u</code> and
      <code>id -g</code>, and that user must own <code>/data</code>. Fix the file, then run
      <code>docker compose up -d</code> again.
    </dd>
    <dt>"Port is already allocated"</dt>
    <dd>
      Another program already uses that port. In the Compose file, change the number on the left of the port mapping
      (for example <code>8097:8096</code>) and open the new port instead.
    </dd>
    <dt>A setting change has no effect</dt>
    <dd>
      Containers read <code>.env</code> only when they are created. Run <code>docker compose up -d</code> again after
      every change; Compose recreates what changed.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="stacks">4. Pick your next stack</h2>
  <p>Each card opens a walkthrough for that service.</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-8 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    {services.map((s) => <ServiceCard service={s} />)}
  </div>

  <h2 id="health">5. Check every port at once</h2>
  <p>
    When you run several stacks, this script from the repository root checks every service port and tells you which
    ones answer:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock title="Check all services" code={`./helpers/check-health.sh`} />
  <p>
    The <a href="/Homelabbing/services#ports">port reference</a> lists the default port and folder of each service.
  </p>
</PageLayout>
